[17592186198482 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198482 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "ea08cd43-7cea-59a6-a6eb-b25618d785bd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199284 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199285 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199284 #edb/ref 1061 44285 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199284 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "77d5039f-8317-56ed-9c58-9503a2ff03cc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199284 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186199466 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199285 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199286 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199285 #edb/ref 1061 44286 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199285 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "04ca83ac-7a80-5eb8-8fb9-98dc863ddddb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199285 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186198381 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199286 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199288 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199286 #edb/ref 1061 44287 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199286 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "68dba314-3daa-559b-9888-94f883e881ef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199286 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186198382 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199287 #edb/ref 1061 44288 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199287 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "af22cc6e-47b4-5a18-a2b1-50d51596813e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199287 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186198383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199288 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199287 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199288 #edb/ref 1061 44289 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199288 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c1d49b55-ca98-5e16-a9de-14e2aaf4fe80" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199288 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186199467 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199466 #edb/ref 1064 "Every division problem can be written as a fraction and vice-versa. In this lesson, we'll extend this idea to division problems involving negative numbers.\n\nTo begin, let's find a fraction that's equivalent to the following division problem:\n\n$$\n(-5) \\div 9\n$$\n\nNotice that we are dividing a negative number ${(\\color{red}{-5}})$ by a positive one $({\\color{blue}{9}}).$ So the result will be negative:\n\n![](images/68/6816c161288078cb818415f78c12133bae8aa85933eae82f74f6601b83142c35.svg)\n\nTo write down the fraction that goes in the box, recall that \n\n$$\n5 \\div 9 = \\dfrac{5}{9}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}(-5)} \\div {\\color{blue}9} = {\\color{red}-\\dfrac{5}{9}}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199466 #edb/ref 1065 7635 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199466 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "331c6a05-6da8-51c8-84ad-b69ff3dd9f56" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199466 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199467 #edb/ref 1064 "What is the missing part of the expression below?\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{41}{8} = \\dfrac{\\bbox[1pt, border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}}{-8}\n$$\n\nA negative number can be obtained if we divide two numbers, one of which is positive and the other negative.\n\nSo, given a negative fraction, we have the following equivalent notations:\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{41}{8} = \\dfrac{-41}{8} = \\dfrac{41}{-8}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we see that the denominator on the right-hand side is negative $({{\\color{red}-8}}).$ Therefore, the missing numerator must be $({{\\color{blue}41}}){:}$\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{41}{8} = \\dfrac{\\bbox[2pt, border: 1pt solid black]{\\color{blue}41}}{-8}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199467 #edb/ref 1065 7636 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199467 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "652d8025-fc3e-56b0-ac6a-10bc44ecaf15" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199467 #edb/ref 1067 "Equivalent Fractions With Negative Numbers" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233078 #edb/ref 1069 "q-103615" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233078 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233078 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to $\\frac{18x-6}{3}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233078 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5277b0dd-a1ab-523e-8996-74d7092ca199" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233078 #edb/ref 1074 "Splitting the fraction into separate terms and then simplifying the fractions in individual terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{18x-6}{3} & = \\\\ \\frac{18x}{3}-\\frac{6}{3} & = \\\\ \\frac{18}{3}\\cdot x-\\frac{6}{3} & = \\\\ 6\\cdot x-2 & = \\\\ 6x-2. & \\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481768 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186482623 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481768 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186482643 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481768 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186482653 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481768 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481768 #edb/ref 1045 "Squaring a Square Root" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481768 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186482623 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481768 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046648 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481768 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050204 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481768 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "5b7f0e4f-3348-59d7-a1d5-4c76f7ddaa47" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481781 #edb/ref 1080 "-36" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481781 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481781 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0561decb-7e57-5d8d-98fc-79752f342f51" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481831 #edb/ref 1080 "121" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481831 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481831 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "14ae428d-aab0-5f78-89db-42a367dcd699" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481853 #edb/ref 1080 "11" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481853 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481853 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1e086f22-20d2-508d-b0b6-19a9f7aabcf9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481880 #edb/ref 1080 "-11" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481880 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481880 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "293ea8b6-0d2c-